2\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source context refers to Voyager 3 as the spacecraft directed to Uranus, not Voyager 2.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-003\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a journey that would take about 4,5 years\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states the journey duration to Uranus.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-004\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Context indicates this refers to Voyager 3's Jupiter encounter, not Voyager 2's.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-005\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The Ur anus encounter's geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states the Uranus encounter geometry was defined by Saturn encounter possibility.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-006\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby.\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Preceding sentence about Saturn encounter geometry indicates the flyby refers to Saturn.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-007\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source identifies Voyager 1, not Voyager 2, as the first spacecraft to fly past Uranus.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-008\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source attributes observations to Voyager 1 on Jan. 31, 1987; claim says Voyager 2 on January 24, 1986.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-009\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Signals statement appears in Voyager 1 context, not Voyager 2 context.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-010\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states this light condition measurement.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-011\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source gives year 1968; claim states 1986.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-012\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_a.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source lists 50,640 km first; claim reverses to 81,500 km first.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-013\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-014\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"three new rings in addition to the \\\"older\\\" eight rings\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source states discovery of three new rings plus eight older rings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-015\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly describes Uranus' magnetic field characteristics.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-016\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"wind speeds in Uranus' atmosphere as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states the wind speed measurements for Uranus' atmosphere.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-017\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly reports evidence of subsurface water lake.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-018\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly describes variability of Uranus' rings.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-019\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states photos of these five moons were returned.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-020\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"five of Uranus' smaller moons\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source identifies these five celestial objects as Uranus' smaller moons.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-021\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers)\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Numerical values match despite formatting difference between periods and commas.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-022\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels.\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source explicitly links Miranda flyby to closest approach in spacecraft's history.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-023\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states Uranus' apparent lack of surface features.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-024\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source directly states the number of astronauts killed in the Challenger accident.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-025\",\n      \"verdict\": \"SUPPORTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"during their space shuttle launch\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source confirms Challenger accident occurred during a space shuttle launch.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim_id\": \"M-026\",\n      \"verdict\": \"CONTRADICTED\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Feb. 28, 1986\",\n      \"evidence_source\": \"source_b.md\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Source gives date as Feb. 28, 1986; claim states January 28, 1986.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```","ttft_ms":